Z||000000||billfredmike24||07-25-2001||11:48 AM||||I was recently given an acoustic guitar as a gift and now need to learn how to play it.  I know nothing at all about guitars or playing music and was wondering if anyone had any good lessons for a beginner or know of any video tapes or instructional aids i should be looking for.  <BR>TIA Mike<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_10.jpg"> <BR>yeah the wheels are ugly, and the stickers, that'll change||||reg||6
Z||000001||JSocha||07-25-2001||12:15 PM||||I have a great book at home.  When I go home for lunch I'll get the name of it and post it here.  May be able to find it at AMAZON or other bookstore.<P>Talks about finger positioning, finger strengthening teqniques, scales, keys, tabulature, methods of tuning, reading music, differnt playing styles from basic backup, blues, lead, jazz, etc.<P>Songman could also probably refer you to some good beginner books.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierobaby||07-25-2001||12:47 PM||||well I don';t know how to play but go to any computer store one that sells software and they should have something to teach you to play guitar I always wanted to try but my brother would kill me if I used his guitar<BR><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="LETTER"> AIM: Fierobaby87<BR>http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero </FONT><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||07-25-2001||01:52 PM||||I can play well, and I live close to you, but that would just be weird.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||07-25-2001||02:17 PM||||learn to play bar chords - once you learn about 3 you can play rythm guitar for just about any song ever written.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JSocha||07-25-2001||02:19 PM||||Title:  Total Guitar<BR>Author: Terry Burrows<BR>ISBN: 0-7607-1166-6 (Hard Back)<BR>ISBN: 0-7607-1168-2 (Paper Back)<P>Published/Sold by: Barnes and Nobel<BR> <A HREF="http://shop.barnesandnoble.com/booksearch/results.asp?WRD=Total+Guitar&userid=6BI4GW6AC8" TARGET=_blank>http://shop.barnesandnoble.com/booksearch/results.asp?WRD=Total+Guitar&userid=6BI4GW6AC8</A> <P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||07-25-2001||02:27 PM||||ok now I gotta explain it<P> at the lowest fret this is an F chord<BR>[hold the three Xs on the lowest fret all down with your index finger - hence the 'bar']<P>X&#0124; &#0124;|XX  <BR>&#0124; &#0124;||XX&#0124; &#0124;|<P>  [hope this lines up right]<P>  and this is an Fminor chord<P>X&#0124; &#0124;XXX<BR>&#0124; &#0124;&#0124; &#0124;&#0124; &#0124;<BR>|XX&#0124; &#0124;|<P> and that covers almost everything. slide it down one fret and the become E and Em<P>slide them up 2 = G<BR> 2 more = A<BR> 2 more = B<BR> 1 more = C<BR> 2 more = D<BR> 2 more and you are back to E<P> the # and b (sharps and flats) are in between<P>  there! now you can play all the major and minor chords. At first you will only be able to hold down the lowest 2 or three strings, then as you practice eventually you will be able to nail all 6 down firmly.||12.145.208.11||reg||1
Z||000007||RiceCooker||07-25-2001||02:28 PM||||Yeah, there's tons of stuff out there.<P>But the thing is you have to WANT to play. It's not easy, especially at the beginning it is kind of frustrating but it is so rewarding when you start to improve.<P>For the record i stink at guitar. I play bass and i also have an acoustic bass.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||07-25-2001||02:29 PM||||oh yeah! first you gotta tune it - got to a Daddys Junky Music store or a used music shop and get a used cheap guitar tuner - one with a needle meter is fine - will cost you about $10. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||billfredmike24||07-25-2001||02:57 PM||||Thanks for all the info. <BR>Jsocha, I think i'll get the total guitar book and see what happens.<BR>Ken, got a tuner, thanks for the tips.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Butter||07-25-2001||04:05 PM||||Pick a Song you REALLY like. Learn the proper chords and wear it out till it sounds like you want it too.  If your not used to chording an acoustic stop just before your fingers want to start bleeding.  Set aside a certain amount of time each day to work on it.  If you have rhythm you can have fun and will know some songs in less than a month.  It also helps to learn certain different riffs you enjoy hearing even if you can not play the whole song.  This will help reduce the monotony of hearing what your trying to mainly learn and ease the stress of your fingers by using different positions.  Certain types of music normally use a common set of fingering positions so pick the book that will fit what type of music you like to want to learn.  Bar chords are ok and need to be learned but I believe this limits ones ability to play well in the long run.  <P>Never met Songman in person but from reading his threads I would speculate he could give some good advise.  Also after working on it for a while ask or get with some more experienced players for more advise.<BR>  <BR>"And if that ain't country I kiss your a$$"<BR>David Allen Coe<p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 07-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Butter||07-25-2001||04:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>learn to play bar chords - once you learn about 3 you can play rythm guitar for just about any song ever written.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You may be able to make a simular noise but it will not be sounding even correctly.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||My7Fieros||07-25-2001||04:20 PM||||I play...I started playing when I was 10...almost 20 years now...(good god).<BR>I own several accoustic/electric guitars (part of that addictive personality I guess, kinda like the Fieros). I tought myself though...never took lessons or used any kinds of books. Any music store will have books w/ chords in it.....you'll need to know them. Good luck! <P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||07-25-2001||04:32 PM||||If you buy sheet music or books of favorite groups, make sure they have TABULATURE notation in them for the guitar licks.  This way you can play some of the guitar licks and practice moving your fingers, sliding and plucking the strings as needed.  Helped me a whole bunch when I was younger by giving me finger strength and dexterity (tips hurt at first until you get some calasus on em).  I haven't played guitar in about 15 years now though since I graduated High School and got married.  My Peavey Mystic, Peavey Renown 400 Amp and Yamaha Accoustic are all in a storage room in my basement right now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Fiero Sam||07-25-2001||06:14 PM||||Speaking from personal experience:I would suggest you avoid mel bay books.<BR>I have a number of his books(I bought one and then was given some others)and they did me no good whatsoever.<BR>Get some chord books and some sheet music books and teach yourself.<BR>Check out free-guitar-tablature.com.I am not sure how accurate it is,but you can give it a shot and see if you want to stick with it before you spend alot of money on tab books.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Urchin||07-25-2001||07:52 PM||||I see you got a new car.  Very nice!  Ya, it could use some new rims though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Sold the old one?  Anyway, on the topic at hand.  When I first started playing I just went into a guitar store and bought a book with the most common used chords in it (showing where to place your fingers).  Then just simply practiced the formation, and going from one chord to the other.  I imagine you could probably find them on the internet.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Songman||07-25-2001||09:46 PM||||I've suggested books to people before and it always amazes me when I look at new beginning guitar books. They all look exactly like the ones my grandfather used when he started teaching me in 1970. I know of course, that all of the chords are still the same and stuff, but they all start out teaching you about tuning and how to hold the pick and the guitar correctly. Always brings back good memories of my grandfather.<P>My recommendation if you want to learn how to actually read and play music is to go with one of the longstanding guitar instructional courses. My grandfather used Mel Bay and the Gibson System to teach me. Mel Bay is about the best you can get in my opinion for beginning stuff.<P>Of course, there is nothing wrong with playing by ear. Some of the greatest guitar players in the world never had a lesson. BUT, I do think that when you are first starting out it doesn't hurt to sign up for an take instructed lessons. There are a lot of bad habits that you can get in to that will only hurt you later if you don't have someone showing you the basics. Most towns I have ever been in have a music store with some guy teaching guitar. Lessons can generally be had for about $25-up for an hour lesson each week.<P>However you decide to do it, good luck to you. I guarantee that you will enjoy it. <P>I'll give you lesson number one for free:<BR>You will feel like giving up because it seems like you will never get it. Don't do it! It takes time for your hands to loosen up and for your fingers to toughen up. Play as much as you can stand and just put up with the sore fingers! It is worth it!<P>That's it for lesson one...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Philphine||07-26-2001||01:56 AM||||the first guitar book i got was called the guitar handbook (i think. been a while since i actually looked at it). good book. not really a lesson book. it covers some better known players, describes common guitar types and brands, covers simple repairs, and dose have a few lessons and theory. just a good overall book to have in a guitar library.<BR>i've had a guitar for nearly 20 yrs. (and that book nearly as long) but i can't play very well. it's a carry over newyears resolution to take lessons.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||07-26-2001||11:43 AM||||wow - so many musicians on PFF - we should start a band!<P> some more basics:<P>1. when you buy strings get light gauge - or even super light if there is such a thing for acoustic (there is for elect). the thinner strings will sound a little more tinny, but they will be MUCH easier to hold down cause you dont wind them  so tight to get the same pitch - makes a HUGE difference when you are starting out. later you can work your way up to regular gauge strings.<P>2. bar chords will get you to the point where you can sit down and strum out a tune and sing along with it - If you are playing the right cord you are 90% there - its the fastest way to get to the point where you will be able to sit down, play and sing a few tunes, and feel like - wow! I can play this thing and it sounds pretty good.<P>3. after a while you will learn other chords, and you wills start picking out melodies and licks, or start picking the strings with your individual fingers instead of strumming them all with a pick - which way you head depends if you want to sound like Bob Dylan or Chet Atkins or Segovia (sic - classical guitarist).<P>4. the important thing is to get to the point where you can play a song you like well enough to play it infront of someone else, then you can say "I play the guitar" - after that is all a matter of how well you play - and that is always something everyone constantly improves on for the rest of their lives.<P>5. take lessons as soon as you can - you will learn a lot faster and be less frustrated in the long run.<P>6. buy new guitar strings at least once a month - even if you didnt play the thing hardly at all, after about a month they, I dont know how to explain it, they go dead - they sound dead, and no matter how good you are they will sound aweful. ||||reg||10
Z||000019||AusFiero||07-26-2001||12:43 PM||||Ahhh memories. I used to play in my school band. Can only play a couple of tunes badly now though. hmmmm ok don't call them tunes. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000020||RiceCooker||07-27-2001||01:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B>My Peavey Mystic, Peavey Renown 400 Amp and Yamaha Accoustic are all in a storage room in my basement right now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I demand you to go get that out and play it. Even if it's just for a couple hours a week. Write a song, you will feel so young and alive (not that you're old and dead).<BR>Write a song about/to your wife so she cannot complain about the god awful noise you're making. j/k<BR>Seriously, i used to watch my friends play in a band and wish i could do the same. Then I was in a bar bathroom one time and this guy just says, "You know, It's NEVER too late"<BR>That's the best thing i ever hear<P>KEEP ON ROCKIN IN A FREE WORLD!!! - Young Neil (Neil Young)||||reg||1
